From the user input, and using the guide below, you will implement a new nuxt
page.

# Hands on guide

Below is a hands on guide on the patterns used in this project to implement a
new nuxt page.

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
// use auth meta to protect the page. if the user is not logged in,
// it will be redirected to the default 404 page
definePageMeta({ auth: true });

const route = useRoute();
// use it:
// route.query.key1
// route.query.key2
// ...
// route.params.key1
// route.params.key2
// ...

// trpc client is available in the app via useNuxtApp()
// check [tRPC Client Guide](mdc:.cursor/rules/trpc-client.mdc) for details if
// needed
const { $trpc } = useNuxtApp();

// ALWAYS use useAsyncData to fetch data as it is SSR safe
const { data, error } = await useAsyncData("items", async () => {
  return await $trpc.items.list.query();
});

// you can use dynamic keys to fetch data based on the route params
const { data: dataDynamic, error: errorDynamic } = await useAsyncData(
  () => `items-${route.params.id}`, // trigger a re-fetch when the route
  async () => await $trpc.items.get.query({ id: route.params.id }),
);

// use Promise.all to fetch multiple queries in parallel. this makes the pages
// load way faster and makes use of trpc batching
const [
  { data: dataItems, error: errorItems },
  { data: dataFiles, error: errorFiles },
] = await Promise.all([$trpc.items.list.query(), $trpc.files.list.query()]);
</script>
```

## Form handling

If you need to handle a form, you can use the following pattern:

Always leave the actual page calls to the top page, if possible. Most
communication should be via emits and props.

Create a `FormItem.vue` component and use it in the page. Check [Form
Rules](mdc:.cursor/rules/ui-form.mdc) for details. Basically, it should, at
least, have a `@submit({ data })` event that will be triggered when the form is
already validated and submitted

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
const { $trpc } = useNuxtApp();
import type { FormItemData } from "~~/components/FormItem.vue";

// always create a onSubmit function that will be used to handle the form
// submission
// prefer using async functions over const functions, because they can be
// referenced above in the code, while the const functions must be already
// defined
async function onSubmit(data: FormItemData) {
  await $trpc.items.create.mutate(data);
}
// if you have multiple forms in the same page, use onSubmit<Entity> to handle
// the form submission
</script>

<template>
  <FormItem
    @submit="
      async (e) => {
        await onSubmit(e.data);
        // refresh some state, for example, a query or table to reflect the
        // changes
        // example: await refreshItems()
      }
    "
  />
</template>
```

## Nested pages

We can make use of nested `<NuxtPage />` components to render the subpages.

For example, the following page structure:

```bash
app/pages/
├── demo
│   ├── page-a.vue  # /demo/page-a
│   ├── page-b.vue  # /demo/page-b
│   └── page-c.vue  # /demo/page-c
└── demo.vue        # /demo
```

The `demo.vue` page will render the subpages in the `<NuxtPage />` component:

```vue
<!-- app/pages/demo.vue -->
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>Demo</h1>
    <!-- will render the subpages page-* -->
    <NuxtPage />
  </div>
</template>
```

## Param validation

If you need to validate the route params, you can use the following pattern:

```vue
<!-- app/pages/demo/[id].vue -->
<script setup lang="ts">
import * as z from "zod";

const route = useRoute();

// used to validate path params: eg: /demo/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
const schemaParams = z.object({ id: z.string().uuid() });
// used to validate query params: eg: /demo?page=1
const schemaQuery = z.object({ page: z.number().default(1).min(1).max(100) });

// definePageMeta is a compiler marco that allows us to add inline stuff to the
// page
definePageMeta({
  // validate the route
  validate: (route) => {
    const resQuery = schemaQuery.safeParse(route.query);
    const resParams = schemaParams.safeParse(route.params);
    if (!resQuery.success)
      throw createError({ status: 400, message: resQuery.error.message });
    if (!resParams.success)
      throw createError({ status: 400, message: resParams.error.message });
    return true;
  },
});
</script>
```
